  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nA fairly potent cold front, sagging south from a low pressure \ncenter straddling the southern Manitoba/Ontario border, \ncontinues to press eastward across MN. Dewpoints drop off \nappreciably going into the Dakotas while the wind shift is \nslowly taking place over far western MN this afternoon. However,\nin advance of this cold front, a narrow moist/warm sector is \nevident with dewpoints into the upper 60s and temperatures into \nthe low-mid 80s. With the front moving across, this is leading \ncolder air aloft into the region despite the low level WAA, \nmaking for steeper lapse rates along with MLCAPE values \napproaching 2000 J/kg. In addition, a modest mid-level shortwave\naloft along with mid-upper level jetting will contribute to \nstronger t-storm development late this afternoon into this \nevening. However, the narrow warm sector along with meager \nsupport aloft will hinder widespread thunderstorm development, \nwhich is generally confirmed by recent CAM runs which do not \nindicate much in the way of coverage. Thus, thunderstorm \ndevelopment looks to commence late this afternoon just \nnorth/east of the TC Metro and press eastward into the evening \nhours, matching the progression of the cold front through the \neastern parts of the WFO MPX coverage area. Cloud cover was a \nlimiting factor in western WI through this morning, but some \nrecovery has allow instability to grow just enough to make \nwestern WI the focus for any strong-severe thunderstorms, which \naligns with the today's SWODY1 updates. The main hazard still \nlooks to be large hail but damaging straight-line winds and a \ntornado cannot be ruled out. Any and all storms look to push out\nof our coverage area by midnight, with western Canadian high \npressure filling in behind the front. \n\nRain-free conditions with lower dewpoints are expected for\nWednesday through Friday as the center of the broad high \npressure airmass lumbers its way eastward across the Upper \nMidwest. Some cloud cover may linger on Wednesday but a \nsignificant dry layer looks to prevent any potential showers \nfrom being realized at the surface. Come Friday night into \nSaturday, a more active weather pattern looks to develop as a \nseries of weak low pressure centers over the Rockies become more\norganized, aided by an enhanced Northern jet stream along with \nmultiple incoming shortwave disturbances. Early Saturday through\nlate Sunday looks to feature multiple rounds of rainfall, \npotentially with scattered thunderstorms, as a warm front takes \nits time moving across the region while deeper swaths of \nmoisture are advected into the region. Rainfall chances, while \ndiminishing, then remain in the forecast into early next week.\n\nTemperatures will remain generally similar day-to-day throughout \nthis forecast period, which is also fairly close to normal \nvalues. Highs throughout the next 7 days will remain in the low-\nmid 80s, but it is the lows that will have more variability due \nto frontal passages and cloud cover hindering radiational \ncooling (or lack thereof enhancing radiational cooling).",
